
10. Megasonic and physical cleaning

Chemistry is not the only way to remove particles.

Cleaning tools can also use physical energy.

Megasonic cleaning uses very high-frequency acoustic energy in liquid to help dislodge particles from the wafer.

Think of it as using controlled microscopic fluid motion to shake contamination loose.

The challenge is again damage.

Too little energy may leave particles behind.

Too much energy can damage fragile device structures.

ACM Research, ticker ACMR, markets its TEBO cleaning technology around controlling megasonic energy to remove small defects while limiting damage to delicate three-dimensional structures.

Whether one vendor's marketing claims translate into durable share is an investment question, but the technical problem is real:

As structures become more fragile, cleaning must become both more powerful and gentler at the same time.

11. Front side, back side and bevel

Most diagrams focus on the device side of the wafer.

But contamination on the back side and outer edge also matters.

A particle on the back of a wafer can prevent it from sitting perfectly flat on a lithography stage or chuck.

That can create focus or alignment problems on the front side.

Particles can also migrate from the backside or bevel into process equipment.

SCREEN has dedicated backside-cleaning equipment, and Tokyo Electron's CELLESTA MS2 is designed to clean both wafer sides while also addressing the wafer edge and bevel.

The lesson is simple:

The entire wafer is part of the manufacturing system, not only the area where transistors are drawn.

12. Where SCREEN fits

SCREEN Holdings, traded in Japan, is one of the most important cleaning-equipment suppliers.

Its semiconductor business sells:

- single-wafer cleaners;
- batch wet stations;
- scrubbers;
- backside cleaners;
- and related surface-processing equipment.

SCREEN states that it holds the world's leading share in single-wafer cleaning equipment.

Its SU-3400 is a 300mm single-wafer cleaner designed around high productivity, particle removal and reduced chemical and utility consumption.

Cleaning is therefore not a side business for SCREEN.

It is a core franchise.

This makes SCREEN one of the clearest public-market companies to remember when you think about wafer cleaning.

13. Where Tokyo Electron fits

Tokyo Electron, or TEL, is another major cleaning supplier.

TEL states that it has the second-largest share of wafer-cleaning systems.

Its CELLESTA family targets advanced 300mm single-wafer cleaning, while its EXPEDIUS platform serves batch wet cleaning.

TEL also offers specialized dry cryogenic cleaning through its ANTARES systems.

TEL's advantage is breadth.

Cleaning sits alongside TEL's positions in coat/develop tracks, deposition, etch and other wafer-processing tools.

That gives TEL exposure to several process steps that can become more difficult as devices scale.

14. Where ACM Research fits

ACM Research is much smaller than SCREEN or Tokyo Electron but has built a meaningful cleaning-equipment business.

Its portfolio includes single-wafer cleaning technologies and hybrid tools that combine batch and single-wafer processing.

Its Ultra C Tahoe system, for example, combines batch processing with single-wafer cleaning chambers for certain sulfuric-peroxide cleaning applications.

The investment angle is different from SCREEN.

SCREEN has a large established cleaning franchise.

ACM is a smaller challenger trying to gain share with differentiated process technology and cost/productivity claims.

That can create more upside if adoption rises, but also more execution, customer-concentration and competitive risk.

15. Why cleaning intensity can rise

Suppose wafer volume grows only modestly.

Cleaning-equipment demand can still grow if each wafer requires more difficult cleaning steps.

Advanced devices can create:

- more material interfaces;
- more sensitive surfaces;
- narrower process windows;
- more fragile three-dimensional structures;
- more opportunities for particles to become killer defects;
- tighter contamination specifications;
- and more difficult drying requirements.

This is process intensity again.

The key question is not only:

How many wafers will the industry produce?

It is also:

How much cleaning complexity is required per wafer?
